M6B11_ROWS = {
    "hook": ["adjust", "no people. `m6-red-cells-normal-sickle-v1.png` at the left, sickle half nearest the centre; on \"approved\" a small coded tick and a grey tag \"approved late 2023\" write under it.", "Hook (an approved treatment and bigger claims): In late 2023, a treatment made with the gene-editing tool CRISPR was approved for the first time, for sickle cell disease.", "CRISPR / plausible", "On the spoken cues: On \"the claims have grown\" three headline strips slide in at the right, one per claim, in plain grey card style (no newspaper branding): \"will wipe out malaria\" (small mosquito plate thumbnail), \"cure every genetic disease\", \"end crop failure\". On \"Some of these could happen\" the strips tilt", "explain", "no people. `m6-red-cells-normal-sickle-v1.png` at the left, sickle half nearest the centre; on \"approved\" a small coded tick and a grey tag \"approved late 2023\" write under it. On \"the claims have grown\" three headline strips slide in at the right, one per claim, in plain grey card style (no newspaper branding): \"will wipe out malaria\" (small mosquito plate thumbnail), \"cure every genetic disease\", \"end crop failure\". On \"Some of these could happen\" the strips tilt slightly and a grey \"?\" writes beside each. On \"plausible\" the tag and strips dim and the heading writes on: \"Plausible, or hype?\" Hold about two seconds.", ["HandText write-on", "Enter slide", "tick", "Plate"], "Hold about two seconds"],
    "crispr-cut": ["new", "the hook clears.", "How CRISPR finds and cuts: CRISPR changes a chosen stretch of DNA using two parts.", "guide RNA / Cas9", "On the spoken cues: A long `SlimStrand` double strand runs across the frame with one stretch faintly marked (no letters). On \"guide RNA\" a short terracotta strand slides in and its bases zip against one strand of the marked stretch (tag \"guide RNA: pairs with the target\"). On \"Cas9\" a translucent lilac-grey enzyme", "explain", "the hook clears. A long `SlimStrand` double strand runs across the frame with one stretch faintly marked (no letters). On \"guide RNA\" a short terracotta strand slides in and its bases zip against one strand of the marked stretch (tag \"guide RNA: pairs with the target\"). On \"Cas9\" a translucent lilac-grey enzyme outline closes around the guide and the DNA (tag \"Cas9 enzyme\", replacing the guide tag). On \"cuts both DNA strands\" two amber nicks appear, one on each strand, and the strand parts slightly. On \"repairs\" the ends rejoin with one small amber gap band where a base was lost (tag \"repair: a few bases added or lost\"). On \"switch that stretch off\" the marked stretch greys. On \"elsewhere\" the camera slides along the strand to a second, similar stretch; the guide hovers and pairs imperfectly (two bases unpaired), and a dashed amber nick appears (tag \"off-target cut\", replacing the repair tag). Hold one second.", ["Enter slide", "Cam lens zoom"], "Hold one second"],
    "sickle-cell-use": ["adjust", "the strand clears.", "The approved use: sickle cell: Now the approved use.", "sickle / foetal haemoglobin", "On the spoken cues: The red cells plate returns, sickle half ringed on \"sickle\", with a small coded narrow vessel outline in which two sickle cells jam (no tag); on \"foetal haemoglobin\" the vessel fades and the plate shrinks to a dim thumbnail at the upper left until \"keeps most red cells from sickling\". On \"foetal", "explain", "the strand clears. The red cells plate returns, sickle half ringed on \"sickle\", with a small coded narrow vessel outline in which two sickle cells jam (no tag); on \"foetal haemoglobin\" the vessel fades and the plate shrinks to a dim thumbnail at the upper left until \"keeps most red cells from sickling\". On \"foetal haemoglobin\" the `GlobinSwitch` draws below: two gene blocks on one slim strip, \"adult\" (with a small amber dot for the sickle base) and \"foetal\". On \"switches the foetal gene off\" a small coded protein glyph labelled \"BCL11A\" settles on the foetal block, which greys. On \"blood-forming stem cells\" the coded flow draws left to right: a small round stem cell glyph cluster, an arrow into `b3a-petri-dish-v1.png` (tag \"edited outside the body\"). On \"cut the DNA\" a mini `CasCut` nick appears on a small strip with a small grey label \"switch control region (red-cell line)\" beside the dish; the label fades on \"With less of it\". At most three bright text items: the gene-block labels are small grey, and only the current tag and the BCL11A glyph label are bright. On \"With less of it\" the BCL11A glyph fades from the foetal block, which brightens, and small round foetal haemoglobin glyphs fill a red cell outline beside the adult ones (tag \"foetal haemoglobin back on\", replacing \"edited outside the body\"). On \"still there\" the amber sickle dot pulses on the adult block (no new tag). On \"keeps most red cells from sickling\" the normal half of the red cells plate brightens. Hold one second.", ["DrawPath", "counter and bar fill", "Plate"], "Hold one second"],
    "evidence-and-catch": ["adjust", "the switch strip shrinks to the top.", "The evidence, and the catch: In the main trial, twenty-nine of the thirty patients followed for long enough had no severe pain crises for at least a year.", "everyone", "On the spoken cues: On \"twenty-nine of the thirty\" a coded grid of 30 small circles draws; 29 fill teal and one stays open (tag \"29 of 30: no severe crises for a year\"). On \"look at what it takes\" the grid dims and a coded three-step strip writes on, one step per cue, each step dimming to grey when the next writes:", "explain", "the switch strip shrinks to the top. On \"twenty-nine of the thirty\" a coded grid of 30 small circles draws; 29 fill teal and one stays open (tag \"29 of 30: no severe crises for a year\"). On \"look at what it takes\" the grid dims and a coded three-step strip writes on, one step per cue, each step dimming to grey when the next writes: \"chemotherapy clears the marrow\" (a small coded bone outline whose red interior empties), on \"weeks in hospital\" \"weeks in hospital\" (a small coded calendar glyph), then the new `m6-infusion-bag-cells-v1.png` at the end with a grey tag \"edited cells returned\". On \"over two million dollars\" a coded price tag glyph reads \"over US$2 million\" (replacing the cells tag). On \"sub-Saharan Africa\" `m8-world-map-v1.png` appears small and sub-Saharan Africa takes a soft fill (tag \"most babies with the disease are born here\", replacing the price tag). On \"everyone\" the strip and map dim to grey and two chips write side by side: \"works in a trial\" (tick) and \"available to everyone\" (grey cross). At most three bright text items at once. Hold one second.", ["HandText write-on", "Mark cross", "ScribbleCircle", "DrawPath", "chip pop", "counter and bar fill", "tick", "Plate"], "Hold one second"],
    "evidence-ladder": ["new", "the map and chips clear.", "How far has the evidence got?: So sort each claim by how far the evidence has got.", "top rung / several directions / one gene / many genes and the environment", "On the spoken cues: The `EvidenceLadder` draws at the left, one rung per question from the bottom, each with its small grey label: \"only an idea\", \"works in the lab\", \"works in animals\", \"human trials\", \"approved and in use\". On \"top rung\" the red cells thumbnail slides onto the top rung. On \"several directions\" three", "explain", "the map and chips clear. The `EvidenceLadder` draws at the left, one rung per question from the bottom, each with its small grey label: \"only an idea\", \"works in the lab\", \"works in animals\", \"human trials\", \"approved and in use\". On \"top rung\" the red cells thumbnail slides onto the top rung. On \"several directions\" three grey chips write to the right of the ladder, one per cue, with a small coded icon each: \"editing inside the body\" (a coded liver outline), \"edited crops\" (a coded leaf), \"DNA screening\" (a coded strip with one ringed band); a fourth small grey chip \"synthetic biology\" sits below them, unspoken and dim. On \"one gene\" the three chips dim and a coded single gene block lights on a strip (tag \"one gene: one stretch to edit\"). On \"many genes and the environment\" the strip shows many small grey bands lighting faintly with a small coded food and exercise icon beside them (tag \"many genes + environment\", replacing the one-gene tag). Hold one second.", ["DrawPath", "chip pop"], "Hold one second"],
    "gene-drive": ["adjust", "the ladder stays at the left, dimmed.", "A proposed use: a gene drive: Malaria, spread by certain mosquitoes, kills hundreds of thousands of people a year.", "half / gene drive / all", "On the spoken cues: `m5x-anopheles-mosquito-v1.png` at the upper right (tag \"malaria: spread by certain mosquitoes\"). On \"about half\" a `ChromoPair` clean diagram panel draws centre: two slim homologue outlines, one with an amber band (tag \"one copy\"), and an `OffspringRow` of eight mosquito glyphs below fills four", "explain", "the ladder stays at the left, dimmed. `m5x-anopheles-mosquito-v1.png` at the upper right (tag \"malaria: spread by certain mosquitoes\"). On \"about half\" a `ChromoPair` clean diagram panel draws centre: two slim homologue outlines, one with an amber band (tag \"one copy\"), and an `OffspringRow` of eight mosquito glyphs below fills four amber and four grey (tag \"about half\", replacing the mosquito tag). On \"gene drive\" the row clears and the term writes on with the gloss \"an allele that copies itself across\". On \"its own CRISPR editor\" a tiny Cas9 outline glyph appears inside the amber band. On \"cuts the matching spot\" the glyph moves to the same position on the other homologue and an amber nick appears there. On \"copying the drive across\" the amber band copies onto the second homologue (both now amber; gloss fades). On \"nearly all\" the `OffspringRow` refills: all eight glyphs amber (tag \"nearly all offspring\"). Hold one second.", ["HandText write-on", "DrawPath", "counter and bar fill", "table build", "Plate"], "Hold one second"],
    "population-crash": ["adjust", "the chromosome panel shrinks to the upper middle.", "Crashing a population: Now aim the drive at a gene that female mosquitoes need to lay eggs.", "crash", "On the spoken cues: On \"a gene that female mosquitoes need\" the amber band takes a small grey label \"inside a female fertility gene\". On \"one copy\" a female glyph with one amber band keeps a small egg cluster icon; on \"two copies\" a female glyph with two amber bands loses it (grey cross on the eggs). On \"the", "explain", "the chromosome panel shrinks to the upper middle. On \"a gene that female mosquitoes need\" the amber band takes a small grey label \"inside a female fertility gene\". On \"one copy\" a female glyph with one amber band keeps a small egg cluster icon; on \"two copies\" a female glyph with two amber bands loses it (grey cross on the eggs). On \"the population can crash\" the `PopLine` draws over generation ticks, flat at first, then falling to the axis. On \"lab cages\" the new `m6-mosquito-cage-v1.png` appears beside the line (tag \"small lab cages: collapse in 7 to 11 generations\"). On \"no gene drive has been released\" the mosquito thumbnail slides onto the ladder's \"works in animals\" rung, and a grey tag \"not released in the wild\" writes beside it, replacing the cage tag. Hold one second.", ["Mark cross", "DrawPath", "tick", "Plate"], "Hold one second"],
    "unknowns": ["new", "the headline strip \"will wipe out malaria\" from the hook returns at the top with its \"?\".", "What could go wrong: So is \"gene drives will wipe out malaria\" plausible?", "Partly / But a mosquito / resistance / far bigger / animals that eat them / borders", "On the spoken cues: On \"Partly\" two small ticks write under it, \"mechanism works in cages\" and \"huge need\". On \"But a mosquito\" the ticks dim and unknown chips write in turn, each dimming when the next writes: on \"resistance\", a mini `ChromoPair` whose cut site band shows a small changed mark and the Cas9 glyph", "explain", "the headline strip \"will wipe out malaria\" from the hook returns at the top with its \"?\". On \"Partly\" two small ticks write under it, \"mechanism works in cages\" and \"huge need\". On \"But a mosquito\" the ticks dim and unknown chips write in turn, each dimming when the next writes: on \"resistance\", a mini `ChromoPair` whose cut site band shows a small changed mark and the Cas9 glyph bounces off (chip \"resistance\"); on \"far bigger\" the cage thumbnail sits small beside a much larger cloud of mosquito glyphs (no new tag); on \"animals that eat them\", a coded food-web fragment (mosquito glyph with two arrows to small bird and fish outlines, a grey \"?\" on each arrow; chip \"ecological effects\"); on \"borders\", a coded map outline with a dashed spreading front crossing a border line (chip \"crosses borders: who decides?\"); on \"call back\", a small coded arrow that cannot return (chip \"hard to reverse\"). At most one chip and the headline are bright at once. Hold one second.", ["DrawPath", "chip pop", "tick"], "Hold one second"],
    "three-questions": ["new", "the three-question board draws to the right of the ladder: three column heads write on one per cue, \"need\", \"feasibility\", \"fairness\", each with its question in small grey under it.", "Need, feasibility, fairness: To judge any future use, ask three questions.", "Need / Feasibility / Fairness / could", "On the spoken cues: On \"Not \\\"it will\\\"\" a line writes under the board: \"it will\" (struck through in grey) then \"it could ... if ...\" (bright), and the three column heads dim to grey. On \"Gene drives could reduce malaria\" the sentence writes in full under it, with \"could\" and \"if\" underlined amber. Hold one second.", "explain", "the three-question board draws to the right of the ladder: three column heads write on one per cue, \"need\", \"feasibility\", \"fairness\", each with its question in small grey under it. On \"Not \\\"it will\\\"\" a line writes under the board: \"it will\" (struck through in grey) then \"it could ... if ...\" (bright), and the three column heads dim to grey. On \"Gene drives could reduce malaria\" the sentence writes in full under it, with \"could\" and \"if\" underlined amber. Hold one second.", ["HandText write-on", "Mark underline", "DrawPath"], "Hold one second"],
    "memory": ["new", "two handwritten lines write on large, one per cue: \"Advanced isn't the same as proven.\" (with the cage thumbnail), \"Need, feasibility, fairness.\" (with three small column icons).", "Memory: Two things to hold on to.", "Advanced isn't the same as proven / need, feasibility and fairness", "On the spoken cues: Hold half a second.", "explain", "two handwritten lines write on large, one per cue: \"Advanced isn't the same as proven.\" (with the cage thumbnail), \"Need, feasibility, fairness.\" (with three small column icons). Hold half a second.", ["HandText write-on"], "Hold half a second"],
    "attempt": ["new", "the ladder and board clear.", "Unseen transfer task (attempt one (editing inside the body)) with every given and demand on screen, a 10 second countdown and no answer cues.", "Two exam questions now. First. A gene-editing treatment", "The question card builds with every given visible and stays on screen; no answer cues appear; the countdown ring runs for 10 seconds after \"Pause here to answer.\" (attempt one (editing inside the body))", "explain", "the ladder and board clear. The card writes on as an exam question, tagged \"HSC-style question, written for HSCScience\": \"A gene-editing treatment for high blood cholesterol is being tested in small, early-stage human trials. The treatment is carried in the blood inside tiny fat particles that are taken up by liver cells. It changes one base in the PCSK9 gene, so the gene no longer codes for a working protein. The PCSK9 protein reduces the liver's ability to remove LDL cholesterol from the blood.\" Demands: \"(a) Explain how this edit could lower the level of LDL cholesterol in the blood. (3 marks)\" \"(b) Identify TWO uncertainties that must be resolved before this becomes a routine treatment. (2 marks)\". Two answer boxes. Nothing answer-bearing: no ladder, no off-target chip, no three-question board. **The countdown ring starts after \"Pause here to answer.\" and runs for 10 seconds.** The whole card stays on screen during the countdown and after it.", ["HandText write-on", "chip pop", "Countdown ring"], "10 second answer-free countdown after \"Pause here to answer.\"; the card stays on screen"],
    "feedback-one": ["new", "the card shrinks to a strip at the top.", "Brief feedback (feedback, item one): the answer, the key reason with the givens underlined, and one crossed classic mistake (owner rule, 16 October).", "remove / else / body cells", "On the spoken cues: (1) On \"stops liver cells making working PCSK9 protein\" \"no longer codes for a working protein\" underlines amber; box (a) fills a three-link chain: \"no working PCSK9 protein\" → \"liver keeps its LDL-removing ability\" → \"less LDL in the blood\" (the second and third links on \"remove\" and \"falls\";", "explain", "the card shrinks to a strip at the top. (1) On \"stops liver cells making working PCSK9 protein\" \"no longer codes for a working protein\" underlines amber; box (a) fills a three-link chain: \"no working PCSK9 protein\" → \"liver keeps its LDL-removing ability\" → \"less LDL in the blood\" (the second and third links on \"remove\" and \"falls\"; \"reduces the liver's ability to remove LDL\" underlines). (2) On \"anywhere else\" box (b) fills \"off-target edits elsewhere in the genome\"; on \"safe for life\" it adds \"long-term safety (permanent edit)\"; \"small, early-stage human trials\" underlines; a grey line under the box lists other accepted answers, \"works in larger groups? cost and access?\". (3) On \"the classic mistake\" the chip \"the patient's children will inherit the edit\" writes on and takes a grey scribble cross, with \"liver cells\" underlined in the strip. Settle on the model answer for about a second.", ["HandText write-on", "Mark cross", "Mark underline", "chip pop", "counter and bar fill"], "Settle on the model answer for about a second"],
    "attempt-two": ["new", "the board clears.", "Unseen transfer task (attempt two (a headline)) with every given and demand on screen, a 10 second countdown and no answer cues.", "Second. A headline says: \"CRISPR will soon cure", "The question card builds with every given visible and stays on screen; no answer cues appear; the countdown ring runs for 10 seconds after \"Pause here to answer.\" (attempt two (a headline))", "explain", "the board clears. The card writes on as an exam question, tagged \"HSC-style question, written for HSCScience\": \"A news headline states: 'CRISPR will soon cure all genetic diseases.'\" Demand: \"Evaluate this claim. Support your answer with examples. (5 marks)\". One lined answer box. Nothing answer-bearing: no ladder, no three-question board. **The countdown ring starts after \"Pause here to answer.\" and runs for 10 seconds.** The whole card stays on screen during the countdown and after it.", ["HandText write-on", "Countdown ring"], "10 second answer-free countdown after \"Pause here to answer.\"; the card stays on screen"],
    "feedback-two": ["new", "the card shrinks to a strip; \"all\" and \"soon\" in the headline underline amber.", "Brief feedback (feedback, item two): the answer, the key reason with the givens underlined, and one crossed classic mistake (owner rule, 16 October).", "many genes", "On the spoken cues: The `EvidenceLadder` returns small at the left with the red cells thumbnail on the top rung. On \"already treat sickle cell\" a tick chip writes \"supported: some single-gene diseases (sickle cell)\". On \"many genes\" a cross chip writes \"not supported: most diseases are many genes + environment\"; on", "explain", "the card shrinks to a strip; \"all\" and \"soon\" in the headline underline amber. The `EvidenceLadder` returns small at the left with the red cells thumbnail on the top rung. On \"already treat sickle cell\" a tick chip writes \"supported: some single-gene diseases (sickle cell)\". On \"many genes\" a cross chip writes \"not supported: most diseases are many genes + environment\"; on \"hard to reach\" a second cross chip \"delivery to most tissues still hard\"; on \"cost\" a third cross chip \"cost and access\". On \"overstated\" a verdict bar writes: \"Verdict: overstated\"; on \"could treat\" a second line writes \"CRISPR could treat some single-gene diseases, if safe and affordable\" with \"could\" and \"if\" underlined. On \"the classic mistake\" the chip \"it's approved, so it works for everyone\" writes on and takes a grey scribble cross. Settle on the model answer for about a second.", ["HandText write-on", "Mark cross", "Mark underline", "chip pop", "counter and bar fill", "tick"], "Settle on the model answer for about a second"],
    "decision-rule": ["new", "four step chips write on in a column, one per cue: \"mechanism: what changes, and how?\", \"rung on the evidence ladder?\", \"need, feasibility, fairness\", \"could ... if ...\".", "Decision rule and handoff: For any future biotechnology, explain the mechanism first: what it changes, and how.", "For any future biotechnology, explain the mechanism first:", "On the spoken cues: A \"Next\" chip slides in at the lower right: \"Next: how biotechnology can change biodiversity\" (small banana and rhino thumbnails). Hold half a second before the notes.", "explain", "four step chips write on in a column, one per cue: \"mechanism: what changes, and how?\", \"rung on the evidence ladder?\", \"need, feasibility, fairness\", \"could ... if ...\". A \"Next\" chip slides in at the lower right: \"Next: how biotechnology can change biodiversity\" (small banana and rhino thumbnails). Hold half a second before the notes.", ["HandText write-on", "Enter slide", "chip pop"], "Hold half a second before the notes"],
    "key-notes": ["new", "Key notes card with the numbered notes, the memory aid in large writing and its condition tag", "Essentials to copy into books", "key notes", "Notes appear then stay still for copying", "hold", "The notes and the memory aid appear and lock", ["Enter reveals"], "Quiet copy hold of about 60 seconds with pause invitation"],
}

M6B12_ROWS = {
    "hook": ["adjust", "no people. Left: `b1-banana-plantation-tr4-v1.png` (healthy and wilting plants); on \"resistance gene\" a small coded `SlimStrand` draws under it and takes one amber inserted band (tag \"gene from a wild banana\").", "Hook (two rescues): Two rescues.", "resistance gene / biodiversity", "On the spoken cues: Right: `m6-northern-white-rhino-v1.png`, with a coded \"2\" badge and two small female symbols on \"two females\"; on \"rhino embryos in the lab\" the `m6-insemination-straw-tank-v1.png` dewar appears small beside it (no tag). On \"stop something dying out\" two small ticks write under the plates. On", "explain", "no people. Left: `b1-banana-plantation-tr4-v1.png` (healthy and wilting plants); on \"resistance gene\" a small coded `SlimStrand` draws under it and takes one amber inserted band (tag \"gene from a wild banana\"). Right: `m6-northern-white-rhino-v1.png`, with a coded \"2\" badge and two small female symbols on \"two females\"; on \"rhino embryos in the lab\" the `m6-insemination-straw-tank-v1.png` dewar appears small beside it (no tag). On \"stop something dying out\" two small ticks write under the plates. On \"biodiversity\" a grey \"?\" writes between them. On \"which level\" and \"compared with\" the tags fade and the heading writes on: \"Which level? Compared with what?\" Hold about two seconds.", ["HandText write-on", "DrawPath", "tick", "Plate"], "Hold about two seconds"],
    "three-levels": ["new", "the hook clears.", "Three levels: Biodiversity isn't one number.", "Genetic diversity / Species diversity / ecosystem diversity", "On the spoken cues: The `LevelsBoard` draws its three rows from the bottom up, one per cue, each with its zoom icon and a short grey gloss: on \"Genetic diversity\", the bottom row \"genetic\" with an allele strip whose rings show several colours (gloss \"alleles in a gene pool\"); on \"Species diversity\", the middle row", "explain", "the hook clears. The `LevelsBoard` draws its three rows from the bottom up, one per cue, each with its zoom icon and a short grey gloss: on \"Genetic diversity\", the bottom row \"genetic\" with an allele strip whose rings show several colours (gloss \"alleles in a gene pool\"); on \"Species diversity\", the middle row \"species\" with four different species outlines (a bee, a bird, a small plant, a beetle; gloss \"species in an area\"); on \"ecosystem diversity\", the top row \"ecosystem\" with three habitat patches (grassland, woodland, wetland; gloss \"habitats across a region\"). Each gloss shrinks to grey when the next row writes. On \"raise one level and lower another\" a sample up arrow appears in the species row and a sample down arrow in the genetic row, then both fade. Hold one second.", ["Cam lens zoom", "DrawPath", "table build"], "Hold one second"],
    "baseline": ["adjust", "the board shrinks to the right.", "Compared with what?: There's a second question: compared with what?", "baseline", "On the spoken cues: The `BaselineSplit` draws: a centre field (the monoculture half of `m6-field-monoculture-mixed-v1.png`, tag \"modified crop field\"). On \"a sprayed conventional field\" a second, coded field outline appears at the left with a small spray-can icon and three insect glyphs; the centre field shows six", "explain", "the board shrinks to the right. The `BaselineSplit` draws: a centre field (the monoculture half of `m6-field-monoculture-mixed-v1.png`, tag \"modified crop field\"). On \"a sprayed conventional field\" a second, coded field outline appears at the left with a small spray-can icon and three insect glyphs; the centre field shows six insect glyphs, and an up arrow joins them (tag \"more insect species\"). On \"the bush it replaced\" the mixed planting half of the same plate appears at the right with many insect glyphs, and a down arrow joins the centre field to it (tag \"far fewer\", replacing \"more insect species\"). On \"Same field, opposite answers\" both arrows pulse together. On \"baseline\" the term writes on with the gloss \"what you compare it with\", and the word \"compared with ...\" moves onto the board as an empty baseline chip. Hold one second.", ["HandText write-on", "DrawPath", "chip pop", "lineage split", "Plate"], "Hold one second"],
    "banana-genetic": ["adjust", "the board returns full width with the crop column active.", "The banana: the genetic level: Back to the bananas.", "genetic", "On the spoken cues: On \"grown from suckers\" the new `m6-banana-wild-vs-cavendish-v1.png` appears small with its seedless Cavendish half ringed (tag \"seedless: grown from suckers\"); the hook's plantation plate stays small beside it, so every plant on screen in this scene is a banana (the strawberry clone row is not", "explain", "the board returns full width with the crop column active. On \"grown from suckers\" the new `m6-banana-wild-vs-cavendish-v1.png` appears small with its seedless Cavendish half ringed (tag \"seedless: grown from suckers\"); the hook's plantation plate stays small beside it, so every plant on screen in this scene is a banana (the strawberry clone row is not used here). On \"the same alleles\" the genetic row's allele strip shows one ring pattern repeated across the row. On \"a resistance gene from a wild banana\" the wild half of the banana plate brightens and a short amber band slides from it into the strip (tag \"gene from a wild banana\", replacing the suckers tag). On \"resisted TR4\" the plantation plate's healthy plants brighten (tag \"approved 2024: a back-up\", replacing the gene tag). On \"still one clone\" the strip's repeated pattern pulses, every copy now carrying the same amber band. On \"Compared with ordinary Cavendish\" the crop column's baseline chip fills \"ordinary Cavendish\" and the genetic cell takes a small up arrow (\"one added resistance trait; crop still one clone\"). On \"Compared with growing many varieties\" a second baseline chip fills \"many varieties\", `b1-varied-seedlings-v1.png` appears small beside it, and the genetic cell under it takes a down arrow (\"still one clone\"). Hold one second.", ["DrawPath", "chip pop", "counter and bar fill", "table build", "Plate"], "Hold one second"],
    "field-species-ecosystem": ["adjust", "the banana thumbnails dim; the crop column's lower cells stay.", "The field: species and ecosystems: Now the field itself.", "species / ecosystem", "On the spoken cues: On \"Bt cotton\" `m6-bt-cotton-v1.png` appears small with b10's two spray-count bars as a thumbnail (\"before Bt\", \"with Bt\"); the species cell takes an up arrow with the baseline chip \"sprayed cotton\" (tag \"more insect species survive\"). On \"tolerate a weedkiller\" the cotton dims and a coded crop row", "explain", "the banana thumbnails dim; the crop column's lower cells stay. On \"Bt cotton\" `m6-bt-cotton-v1.png` appears small with b10's two spray-count bars as a thumbnail (\"before Bt\", \"with Bt\"); the species cell takes an up arrow with the baseline chip \"sprayed cotton\" (tag \"more insect species survive\"). On \"tolerate a weedkiller\" the cotton dims and a coded crop row with a few wildflower and weed glyphs between the plants appears (tag \"herbicide-tolerant crop\"); on \"spray the whole crop\" a coded spray drifts across and the weed and wildflower glyphs grey out, then the small insect glyphs above them leave (tag \"fewer weeds and wildflowers, fewer insects\", replacing the herbicide tag); on \"compared with fields where some weeds survive\" the species cell takes a second, down arrow beside the first, with its own small baseline chip \"fields where some weeds survive\". On \"ecosystem\" the ecosystem row lights: a coded landscape strip with a farm block and a bush block; on \"less bush cleared\" the farm block stays the same size while a dashed \"would-be farmland\" outline over the bush fades (tag \"could spare land\"); on \"only if that land is actually protected\" the ecosystem cell takes a grey \"?\" with a small condition line \"if land is protected\". At most one tag and the active cell are bright. Hold one second.", ["DrawPath", "chip pop", "table build", "Plate"], "Hold one second"],
    "rhino-rescue": ["adjust", "the crop column dims to grey; the conservation column opens with `m6-northern-white-rhino-v1.png` at its top.", "The rhino rescue: Now the rhino.", "surrogate", "On the spoken cues: On \"The last male died in 2018\" a small grey tag \"2018: last male died\" writes and fades. On \"Najin and Fatu\" two coded name chips sit under the plate. The `RescueFlow` draws left to right below the board, one step per cue: on \"collect eggs from Fatu\", a small egg glyph from the Fatu chip; on", "explain", "the crop column dims to grey; the conservation column opens with `m6-northern-white-rhino-v1.png` at its top. On \"The last male died in 2018\" a small grey tag \"2018: last male died\" writes and fades. On \"Najin and Fatu\" two coded name chips sit under the plate. The `RescueFlow` draws left to right below the board, one step per cue: on \"collect eggs from Fatu\", a small egg glyph from the Fatu chip; on \"fertilise them in the lab\", `m6-icsi-v1.png` small; on \"sperm frozen\", the dewar plate small with a grey label \"banked sperm\"; on \"freeze the embryos\", a row of small coded embryo circles; on \"surrogate\", a second rhino plate, smaller and tinted slightly warmer, labelled in code \"southern white rhino (surrogate)\", with an arrow from the embryos to it, and the term \"surrogate\" writes on with the gloss \"carries another's embryo\". On \"dozens\" the embryo row counts up to a coded \"39\" (tag \"39 embryos (2026)\"), just before the surrogate arrow draws; on \"no northern white pregnancy has lasted yet\" the arrow to the surrogate takes a grey \"not yet\". Hold one second.", ["HandText write-on", "ScribbleCircle", "DrawPath", "chip pop", "table build", "Plate"], "Hold one second"],
    "rhino-levels": ["new", "the rescue flow shrinks; the conservation column's cells fill on their cues.", "Tracing the rhino: Trace the levels.", "subspecies / species / genetic / ecosystem", "On the spoken cues: On \"subspecies\" the baseline chip \"doing nothing\" fills and a tag writes: \"could preserve the subspecies and some of its genetic diversity\". On \"species\" the species cell takes a grey \"?\", not an up arrow, with the condition line \"adds a species to an area only if white rhinos are absent from that", "explain", "the rescue flow shrinks; the conservation column's cells fill on their cues. On \"subspecies\" the baseline chip \"doing nothing\" fills and a tag writes: \"could preserve the subspecies and some of its genetic diversity\". On \"species\" the species cell takes a grey \"?\", not an up arrow, with the condition line \"adds a species to an area only if white rhinos are absent from that baseline area\". On \"one female's eggs and a few males' sperm\" the genetic row's ring cluster (b08's gene-pool style) draws small: one female's two rings and three males' rings, a handful of colours only; on \"bottleneck\" the cluster passes through a narrow coded bottleneck outline and the genetic cell takes a down arrow (tag \"bottleneck from the start\", replacing the subspecies tag; the species condition line dims). On \"a second route\" a dashed branch draws from the dewar: about twelve small coded cell circles, each a different ring colour, pass through an arrow labelled small \"stem cells\" to egg and sperm glyphs; the gene-pool cluster gains more colours, and the down arrow lightens to a grey \"?\" with the condition line \"wider if more founders\" (tag \"a second route, still in research\"); the bottleneck outline and the \"stem cells\" label dim to grey as the tag writes, so at most three text items are bright. On \"decades away\" the ecosystem row's grassland patch lights faintly and the ecosystem cell takes a grey \"?\" (\"decades away\"). Hold one second.", ["ScribbleCircle", "DrawPath", "chip pop", "counter and bar fill", "table build"], "Hold one second"],
    "yield-not-a-level": ["new", "the full board, both columns at equal weight with their arrows and baseline chips.", "Yield isn't a level: Notice what isn't a level: yield.", "yield / not a level of biodiversity / isn't a gain at every level / Strong answers / separately / clearing habitat", "On the spoken cues: On \"yield\" a chip \"more yield\" slides toward the board and stops outside it, finding no row; it takes a grey scribble cross (caption \"not a level of biodiversity\"). On \"isn't a gain at every level\" the conservation column's three cells pulse in turn (all three grey \"?\", each with its condition). On", "explain", "the full board, both columns at equal weight with their arrows and baseline chips. On \"yield\" a chip \"more yield\" slides toward the board and stops outside it, finding no row; it takes a grey scribble cross (caption \"not a level of biodiversity\"). On \"isn't a gain at every level\" the conservation column's three cells pulse in turn (all three grey \"?\", each with its condition). On \"Strong answers\" a model sentence writes under the board: \"This could preserve the northern white rhino subspecies and some of its genetic diversity. It would add a species to a particular area only if white rhinos are absent from that baseline area.\" with \"subspecies\", \"a species\" and \"baseline area\" underlined amber. On \"separately\" two small grey chips write beside it: \"genetic: bottleneck\" and \"ecosystem: decades away\". On \"clearing habitat\" the sentence and chips dim and a small coded landscape patch beside the board loses its trees to a bare block (tag \"on land: habitat clearing is the biggest driver\"); on \"whatever the crop\" a second, coded field of a different crop clears the same way beside it; on \"the farming it replaces\" the crop column's baseline chip flashes \"conventional farming\". Hold one second.", ["Mark cross", "Mark underline", "DrawPath", "chip pop", "table build"], "Hold one second"],
    "memory": ["adjust", "two handwritten lines write on large, one per cue: \"The banana problem returns.\" (with the clone row thumbnail and the bull plate thumbnail `b3b-dairy-bull-side-v1.png` for \"one top sire\"), \"Which level? Compared with what?\" (with the three row icons and an empty baseline chip).", "Memory: Two things to hold on to.", "The banana problem returns / which level, and compared with what?", "On the spoken cues: On \"one disease can hit many\" a small grey tag writes under the first line, \"copies narrow the gene pool\", then fades. Hold half a second.", "explain", "two handwritten lines write on large, one per cue: \"The banana problem returns.\" (with the clone row thumbnail and the bull plate thumbnail `b3b-dairy-bull-side-v1.png` for \"one top sire\"), \"Which level? Compared with what?\" (with the three row icons and an empty baseline chip). On \"one disease can hit many\" a small grey tag writes under the first line, \"copies narrow the gene pool\", then fades. Hold half a second.", ["HandText write-on", "DrawPath", "chip pop", "table build", "Plate"], "Hold half a second"],
    "attempt": ["new", "the board clears: no rows, arrows or baseline chips.", "Unseen transfer task (attempt one (a crop and its wild relative)) with every given and demand on screen, a 10 second countdown and no answer cues.", "Two exam questions now. First. A crop has", "The question card builds with every given visible and stays on screen; no answer cues appear; the countdown ring runs for 10 seconds after \"Pause here to answer.\" (attempt one (a crop and its wild relative))", "explain", "the board clears: no rows, arrows or baseline chips. The card writes on as an exam question, tagged \"HSC-style question, written for HSCScience\": \"A crop plant has been genetically modified to tolerate a herbicide (weedkiller). The crop is grown beside a small population of a wild relative that can cross-pollinate with it. Farmers spray the herbicide along the field edges, where the wild plants grow.\" Demand: \"Explain how this could reduce the genetic diversity of the wild population. (4 marks)\". One lined answer box. Nothing answer-bearing: no gene-pool rings, no arrows. **The countdown ring starts after \"Pause here to answer.\" and runs for 10 seconds.** The whole card stays on screen during the countdown and after it.", ["HandText write-on", "Mark cross", "DrawPath", "chip pop", "table build", "Countdown ring"], "10 second answer-free countdown after \"Pause here to answer.\"; the card stays on screen"],
    "feedback-one": ["new", "the card shrinks to a strip at the top.", "Brief feedback (feedback, item one): the answer, the key reason with the givens underlined, and one crossed classic mistake (owner rule, 16 October).", "gene flow / common", "On the spoken cues: A small coded panel draws: crop rows at the left, a wild plant cluster at the right with b08-style coloured rings. (1) On \"gene flow\" \"can cross-pollinate with it\" underlines amber; pollen dots drift from the crop to the wild plants and one amber ring joins the wild cluster (chip \"gene flow by", "explain", "the card shrinks to a strip at the top. A small coded panel draws: crop rows at the left, a wild plant cluster at the right with b08-style coloured rings. (1) On \"gene flow\" \"can cross-pollinate with it\" underlines amber; pollen dots drift from the crop to the wild plants and one amber ring joins the wild cluster (chip \"gene flow by pollen\"). (2) On \"survive and breed\" \"spray the herbicide along the field edges\" underlines; a coded spray crosses the wild cluster; plants without the amber ring grey out and the amber ring doubles over two quick ticks (chip \"selection: tolerance becomes common\"). (3) On \"swamp\" \"small population\" underlines; more crop rings flow in and several local colours drop out of the cluster (chip \"local alleles lost\"). (4) On \"the classic mistake\" the chip \"a crop's genes can't reach a different population\" writes on and takes a grey scribble cross. Settle on the model answer for about a second.", ["HandText write-on", "Mark cross", "Mark underline", "DrawPath", "chip pop", "tick", "table build"], "Settle on the model answer for about a second"],
    "attempt-two": ["adjust", "the board clears.", "Unseen transfer task (attempt two (restoring a tree)) with every given and demand on screen, a 10 second countdown and no answer cues.", "Second. In the early twentieth century, a fungal", "The question card builds with every given visible and stays on screen; no answer cues appear; the countdown ring runs for 10 seconds after \"Pause here to answer.\" (attempt two (restoring a tree))", "explain", "the board clears. The card writes on as an exam question, tagged \"HSC-style question, written for HSCScience\", with the new `m6-chestnut-burr-leaf-v1.png` small at its upper left: \"American chestnut trees were once common in forests across eastern North America. In the early twentieth century, a fungal disease (chestnut blight) killed billions of them. Wild chestnuts survive, mostly as small shoots growing from old roots. Scientists have proposed planting chestnut trees genetically modified with a gene that gives resistance to the fungus, crossed with surviving wild chestnuts.\" Demand: \"Evaluate the claim that this program will improve biodiversity. (5 marks)\". One lined answer box. Nothing answer-bearing: no levels board, no arrows. **The countdown ring starts after \"Pause here to answer.\" and runs for 10 seconds.** The whole card stays on screen during the countdown and after it.", ["HandText write-on", "DrawPath", "Plate", "Countdown ring"], "10 second answer-free countdown after \"Pause here to answer.\"; the card stays on screen"],
    "feedback-two": ["new", "the card shrinks to a strip.", "Brief feedback (feedback, item two): the answer, the key reason with the givens underlined, and one crossed classic mistake (owner rule, 16 October).", "Species level / Genetic level / if crossed widely / Ecosystem level / partly supported / the classic mistake", "On the spoken cues: A small `LevelsBoard` draws beneath it with one column and the baseline chip \"forests without chestnuts\". (1) On \"Species level\" \"were once common\" and \"killed billions\" underline amber; the species cell takes an up arrow (\"tree returns\"). (2) On \"Genetic level\" \"crossed with surviving wild", "explain", "the card shrinks to a strip. A small `LevelsBoard` draws beneath it with one column and the baseline chip \"forests without chestnuts\". (1) On \"Species level\" \"were once common\" and \"killed billions\" underline amber; the species cell takes an up arrow (\"tree returns\"). (2) On \"Genetic level\" \"crossed with surviving wild chestnuts\" underlines; the genetic cell takes an up arrow with the condition \"if crossed widely\", and a coded strip of one repeated ring pattern sits beside it, crossed in grey (\"a few clones: narrow\"). (3) On \"Ecosystem level\" the ecosystem cell takes a grey \"?\" (\"food tree back; other effects uncertain\"). (4) On \"partly supported\" a verdict bar writes: \"Verdict: partly supported. Species: likely gain. Genetic: gain only if bred widely. Ecosystem: uncertain.\" (5) On \"the classic mistake\" the chip \"it saves a species, so every level improves\" writes on and takes a grey scribble cross. Settle on the model answer for about a second.", ["HandText write-on", "Mark cross", "Mark underline", "DrawPath", "chip pop", "counter and bar fill"], "Settle on the model answer for about a second"],
    "decision-rule": ["new", "four step chips write on in a column, one per cue: \"case + baseline (compared with what?)\", \"genetic: alleles in the gene pool\", \"species: species in the area\", \"ecosystem: habitats across the region\", with a bracket \"which way, and why?\".", "Decision rule and handoff: So when a question asks how a biotechnology changes biodiversity, name the case and the baseline.", "So when a question asks how a biotechnology", "On the spoken cues: A \"Next\" chip slides in at the lower right: \"Next: weighing benefits against biodiversity risks\" (a small coded balance glyph). Hold half a second before the notes.", "explain", "four step chips write on in a column, one per cue: \"case + baseline (compared with what?)\", \"genetic: alleles in the gene pool\", \"species: species in the area\", \"ecosystem: habitats across the region\", with a bracket \"which way, and why?\". A \"Next\" chip slides in at the lower right: \"Next: weighing benefits against biodiversity risks\" (a small coded balance glyph). Hold half a second before the notes.", ["HandText write-on", "Enter slide", "chip pop"], "Hold half a second before the notes"],
    "key-notes": ["new", "Key notes card with the numbered notes, the memory aid in large writing and its condition tag", "Essentials to copy into books", "key notes", "Notes appear then stay still for copying", "hold", "The notes and the memory aid appear and lock", ["Enter reveals"], "Quiet copy hold of about 60 seconds with pause invitation"],
}
